      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=7\\cos t\\,i+3\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/88/88907a8e0d33f889ae4fe3723a239b904a302f41fa294fc372e9d73cb049128a.png)",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -7\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}3\\sin t\\cdot(-7\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{21}{2}\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{21}{2}t-\\frac{21}{4}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(21 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=21 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=21 \\pi.$",
